A||||18||daved||V6 Wierd symptoms||||1||
Z||000000||daved||09-07-2000||09:40 PM||||My 86GT Auto quit on the way to work yesterday; it just gave a cough and a little backfire, then died.  It started up right away, but would only go at walking pace.  If I tried to open the throttle, she would give a little cough, backfire and die.  Starts right back up again no problem....but with the same symptoms.<BR>   Got it home, checked codes...none found.  Checked fuel pressure at the rail...o/k.  Checked timing in diagnostic mode...spot on.  Took it for a drive in diagnostic mode.  It runs o/k at idle, pre-ignition (pinging like a bucket of bolts in the back!) as it accelerates, and is o/k at speed, though with very poor response to more power.<BR>   The "check engine light" flashes once per sec. (normal closed loop operation) while at a constant speed, changes to 'always on' under hard acceleration, and off when engine braking.  In short, everything appears normal to the ECM.<BR>   These past few weeks it had been harder starting, and the first minute or so had symptoms of 'insuffcient choke'.  Perhaps with all this info, someone on the Forum will recognize the symptoms, and offer a cure?  Thanks in advance,  Dave||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phil||09-07-2000||09:46 PM||||My 85GT did just about the same thing -replaced the ignition module and then it was all was fixed.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Gold-86SE||09-08-2000||10:14 AM||||<BR>I'd replace the fuel filter (if it has not be done recently) and see what happens.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Gold-86SE (Fiero Collector)||||reg||1
Z||000003||ThatRickGuy||09-08-2000||11:38 AM||||Check the easy stuff first, but my guess is on the timing chain. when they get old and loose, they can/will jump a tooth. The timing will appear fine, it'll run at idle because the timing is fully retarded, but as soon as you hit the gas it's trying to fire earlier, but since the timing chain is off a tooth it fires way to early (or too late) and will die (just like you said, chough, spit, die) Happened to me once a few years ago in the grand prix, made it about 5 miles while off one tooth, came to a stop sign, it jumped tooth number 2 and was done.||||reg||1
Z||000004||daved||09-09-2000||02:38 PM||||Phil, I replaced the ignition module, rotor & cap, but no change.  It runs, but no power at all!<BR>ThatRickGuy....dont think it can possible be the timing chain because the timing is spot on at idle.  It would show as really retarded if it had jumped a tooth (I think!).<BR>  Anyone have any idea's of where to go from here?  Plugged Cat?   Ignition Coil?   Help!<BR>  Dave||||reg||1
Z||000005||contra||09-09-2000||03:37 PM||||you could check the EGR to see if it's messed up. Also check if there is any vaccum leaks, or any sort of leaks would be bad. Also check the transmission isn't having problems. I'm not an expert on cars, nor do i know if what i'm talking about could have anything to do with the problem, but from my personal experience, EGR and Vaccum Leaks cause my idle to be screwy and vaccum cause my car to die once||||reg||1
Z||000006||Mark||09-09-2000||04:47 PM||||Have you checked the quality of the spark using a spare plug?  I'm thinking bad coil.  Easy enough to check too.||||reg||1
Z||000007||myblack86GT||09-09-2000||05:58 PM||||Check your Distributor. I had the same problem, something foreign had bent the distributor prongs and it was sticking. Replaced it and she runs great.||||reg||1
Z||000008||mrfiero||09-09-2000||06:42 PM||||Another possibility is the catalytic convertor has plugged itself up.  Jack the car into the air and place on jackstands.  Crawl underneath and pound the cat with your fist.  If you hear something rattling around then you know the cat is bad.  <P>This happened on my very first Fiero way back in 1990.  It was an '88 GT with 25K miles on it, but the cat plugged itself up solid.  I had no power and couldn't get above 10 MPH.  <P>Good luck....I hope you figure it out soon.||||reg||1
Z||000009||daved||09-10-2000||01:48 PM||||Thanks for the tips guys.  Mark..good idea on the spark plug, should have thought of that right away.  But it does give a nice bright blue spark, so I guess the coil is probably o/k.  Might buy one anyway just to eliminate that as a cause.<BR>  MyBlack...dont really know what you mean by the "prongs" in the dist., perhaps the ones inside the cap?  Or the reluctor coil (timer core) stator?  In any case, I've had the thing apart so many times, I'm sure nothing is binding.  The spark is good, which is a pretty good indicator that the pick-up coil is working o/k.  I will check that lot one more time....just in case!<BR>  MrFiero...you may be right; a plugged Cat.  I should limp to a muffler shop and get them to check it.  It had been rattling inside for some time when cold.  Maybe something finally let go, causing the original stall.  Now I just have a very restricted exhaust.<BR>  Let you know how it turns out.  Dave||||reg||1
Z||000010||Wade87GT||09-11-2000||03:01 PM||||Hey Daved,<BR>Please let us know what the final fix was. These symptoms seem to be coming up quite a bit lately. Mine (supposedly) was a bad fuel pump, 20 PSI instead of the needed 45-47. My plugged cat, a few years ago, caused boil-over at above 2500 RPM. Too much back pressure from a clogged exhaust can boil the coolant in the heads, resulting in overheating. Does this make sense?||||reg||5
Z||000011||daved||09-12-2000||12:32 PM||||Wade...Thanks for the advice. I have replaced the coil now, and it does run a little better, but still way low on power. Think I will try the fuel pump pressure again, as I only checked it idle.  If I rev it up and the pressure drops significantly I would assume the pump has partially failed.<BR>  Went to the Muffler Man shop and asked if they would test my Cat., but they said they hadnt the tools! (I asked them "you are a muffler shop?" LOL).  Don't think I will let those clowns touch my baby!. <BR>  So I think I will also try disconnecting the EGR tube to the intake manifold and rig up a gauge to it.  I expect to see around 3psi max., (when I lift the EGR valve pintle by pressing up on the diaphragm) so that will tell me what I need to know for the exhaust restriction.  Keep you posted, Dave||||reg||1
Z||000012||daved||09-13-2000||11:16 PM||||Just an update for those following my problem.  Put a 0-60psi gauge on the fuel rail (not bled, and no idea of the gauge accuracy) and this is what I got;<BR>   43 psi on key on<BR>   37 running @ 3000rpm<BR>   47 when blipping the throttle<BR>Given the unkown nature of the gauge, and the lack of bleeding, I would say these results tell me that the fuel pump pressure is o/k.  Haynes says 40.5 to 47, so it could be just a tiny bit low.  Comments?||||reg||1
Z||000013||Blaster||09-14-2000||12:01 AM||||I have been having nearly the same problem.. damn car is driving me crazy. Did you try checking if you have spark leakage? Anyways, mine is doing it when I get on it. It feels like it wants to go until I get to around 3500rpms and it just lets loose and feels fine until I shift into the next gear and it starts the studdering crap again.  I just can't figure it out. I had it die on me the other day while going down the street.. just shut off. Pulled off the road and it started right back up. I know it isn't my cat because I don't have one. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I put a new cap, coil, and the dist is a year old. I had the control module tested and it was ok. I got 8mm wires and acdelco plugs. I just can't figure it out. Maybe you can pick up something off of what got/did. Oh yeah, if your cat is clogged, then your manifolds will most likely be glowing. I know mine sure as hell did. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Later.||||reg||1
Z||000014||btoth||09-14-2000||10:43 AM||||when my catalytic converter died the car had all of the same problems:  stalling, wouldn't accelerate, wouldn't shift when it was supposed to, etc. etc.  took the car to midas and got a new cat. and it runs good as new now.  so i would definatly try to get the cat. checked out.  (btw:  i had no idea that my cat. was clogged, exhaust sounded fine and smelled ok and the car idled just fine it just didn't want to go anywhere.  it was just the one thing i hadn't replaced on my car yet!)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||3
Z||000015||daved||09-15-2000||09:42 AM||||Well I measured the exhaust system backpressure last night.  I found it easier to remove the EGR valve and make a small 1/4" thick aluminum plate to fit in its place.  Drilled a hole in the exhaust port area of the plate and screwed in a 0-30 psi gauge.  The gauge never read much above zero to 1 psi pulses, let alone 3.5 psi!  So I guess that isn't the problem.<BR>  I had the intake snorkle off to make it easier to get at the EGR.  It makes a terrific sucking sound from the throttle at idle!  I would take this to mean that I have good vacuum = no mechanical timing problems = good compression?  Now what?  Any advice, Dave||||reg||1
Z||000016||pollock||09-15-2000||10:09 AM||||Check the TPS and the O2 sensor, if you haven't already.  I have no idea what the specs should be.||||reg||1
Z||000017||daved||09-22-2000||01:16 PM||||The Saga continues....Checked the MAT air temp. sensor = 4.27k Ohms @ 10 deg C.<BR>Coolant temp. sensor = 2.15k Ohms @ 20 deg C.<BR>  I had to estimate the temperature, but even so, both sensors appear to read normal at the cold condition; a pretty good indication that they are o/k I would think.<BR>  Don't know why the throttle Pos. sensor would exhibit my symptoms; I did move the 'tang' around at idle, which caused the engine to eventually stall.  Would this indicate that it at least is working?  I get absolutely no hunting at highway speeds, which would have caused me to suspect this component.<BR>  So I figure that it is down to the MAP sensor, or the ECM.  I am going to buy a new MAP sensor today, and if that does not solve my problem, I shall take the car to Canadian Tire to get a diagnostic printout of what the ECM is seeing. ($65 Cdn.)  Maybe I should have done that right away...but I thought with my limited experience and the Forums help, it would be an easy fix!<BR>  Any further advice out there?||||reg||1
Z||000018||fiero56||09-22-2000||08:47 PM||||This was my first time reading this thread and I had one sensor in mind the entire time. MAP sensor. A faulty MAP sensor will allow an engine to idle perfectly, but once a load is placed on the engine it sputters terribly or dies. Usually sets a code though. My only other thought is serveral bad plug wires, but seems to coincidental for several to become faulty at once. <P>Remember, electrical parts are not returnable.||||reg||1
